{"content_hash": "5ddfb74eedefe6f1", "version_id": 504, "article_id": 51, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Lancet warns AI and nuclear war could end humanity by 2100", "title_zh": "柳叶刀警告：AI滥用与核战争或让人类在2100年前灭绝", "has_translation": true, "topic": "tech", "source": "guardian_tech", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/11/nuclear-war-and-malicious-use-of-ai-threaten-end-of-humanity-by-2100-says-lancet-assessment", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1729284045177-7a40238e4cd0?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8NHx8bnVjbGVhciUyMHdhciUyMG1hbGljaW91c3xlbnwwfDB8fHwxNzkxNjc3ODg3fDA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Emanuel Kypreos on Unsplash", "author": "Emanuel Kypreos", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@mankip67?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/a-large-cloud-of-smoke-is-billowing-out-of-the-sky-XySaPZljJ54?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(260, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(298, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "6a7c012390e8"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-10", "word_count": 233, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "A new global assessment says humans could become extinct by 2100 because of the malicious use of AI or nuclear conflict. The Lancet Commission lists 17 major threats to health, but says each one can still be tackled.", "lead_zh": "一项新的全球评估指出，到2100年，人类可能因AI被恶意使用或核冲突而灭绝。柳叶刀委员会列出了17项健康重大威胁，但表示每一项都仍可应对。", "preview": "A new report by the Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health warns that humans could die out by 2100. The two biggest dang", "preview_zh": "柳叶刀21世纪全球健康威胁委员会的一份新报告警告，人类可能在2100年前灭绝。两大危险是AI被恶意使用（包括生物武器）和核冲突，后者可能遮蔽阳光并引发饥荒。报告称这些风险仍然不大，但一旦发生，后果将是灾难性的。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 492, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/51/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 99.75, "size_bytes": 602208, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/504.json", "paragraphs": ["A new report by the Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health warns that humans could die out by 2100. The two biggest dangers are the malicious use of AI, including biological weapons, and nuclear conflict, which could block sunlight and cause famine. The report says these risks are still unlikely, but their possible effects would be apocalyptic.", "The commission identified 17 major threats to human health over the next 75 years. Besides AI and nuclear war, the list includes air pollution, antimicrobial resistance, childhood malnutrition, climate breakdown, declining overseas aid and high alcohol use. It also names high blood pressure, income inequality, low education, pandemics, obesity, poor mental health, smoking and unhealthy ageing.", "To be included, each threat had to cause many lost healthy years and also have possible solutions. The experts modelled how many years of good health might be lost due to sickness, disability or early death. Limiting global heating to about 1.8C instead of 4.4C could prevent 1.89 billion years of healthy life being lost, the report found.", "The commission was made up of 35 experts, and its findings will be shared at the World Health Summit in Berlin. The assessment will be updated every year. The authors urged leaders to act before risks become crises, by investing in technology and strengthening health systems. Decisions made today, they say, will shape the health of the next generation."], "paragraphs_zh": ["柳叶刀21世纪全球健康威胁委员会的一份新报告警告，人类可能在2100年前灭绝。两大危险是AI被恶意使用（包括生物武器）和核冲突，后者可能遮蔽阳光并引发饥荒。报告称这些风险仍然不大，但一旦发生，后果将是灾难性的。", "该委员会确定了未来75年人类健康的17项重大威胁。除AI和核战争外，名单还包括空气污染、抗微生物药物耐药性、儿童营养不良、气候崩溃、海外援助减少和过量饮酒。报告还列出了高血压、收入不平等、教育程度低、大流行病、肥胖、心理健康差、吸烟和不健康老龄化。", "要入选名单，每项威胁必须造成大量健康寿命损失，同时也要有可行的解决方案。专家们模拟了因疾病、残疾或过早死亡可能损失多少健康年数。报告发现，将全球升温限制在约1.8摄氏度而非4.4摄氏度，可避免损失18.9亿健康寿命年。", "该委员会由35位专家组成，研究结果将在柏林世界卫生峰会上分享。评估将每年更新。作者敦促领导人在风险演变成危机前采取行动，投资技术创新并加强卫生系统。他们表示，今天做出的决定将塑造下一代的健康。"], "body": "A new report by the Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health warns that humans could die out by 2100. The two biggest dangers are the malicious use of AI, including biological weapons, and nuclear conflict, which could block sunlight and cause famine. The report says these risks are still unlikely, but their possible effects would be apocalyptic.\n\nThe commission identified 17 major threats to human health over the next 75 years. Besides AI and nuclear war, the list includes air pollution, antimicrobial resistance, childhood malnutrition, climate breakdown, declining overseas aid and high alcohol use. It also names high blood pressure, income inequality, low education, pandemics, obesity, poor mental health, smoking and unhealthy ageing.\n\nTo be included, each threat had to cause many lost healthy years and also have possible solutions. The experts modelled how many years of good health might be lost due to sickness, disability or early death. Limiting global heating to about 1.8C instead of 4.4C could prevent 1.89 billion years of healthy life being lost, the report found.\n\nThe commission was made up of 35 experts, and its findings will be shared at the World Health Summit in Berlin. The assessment will be updated every year. The authors urged leaders to act before risks become crises, by investing in technology and strengthening health systems. Decisions made today, they say, will shape the health of the next generation.", "body_zh": "柳叶刀21世纪全球健康威胁委员会的一份新报告警告，人类可能在2100年前灭绝。两大危险是AI被恶意使用（包括生物武器）和核冲突，后者可能遮蔽阳光并引发饥荒。报告称这些风险仍然不大，但一旦发生，后果将是灾难性的。\n\n该委员会确定了未来75年人类健康的17项重大威胁。除AI和核战争外，名单还包括空气污染、抗微生物药物耐药性、儿童营养不良、气候崩溃、海外援助减少和过量饮酒。报告还列出了高血压、收入不平等、教育程度低、大流行病、肥胖、心理健康差、吸烟和不健康老龄化。\n\n要入选名单，每项威胁必须造成大量健康寿命损失，同时也要有可行的解决方案。专家们模拟了因疾病、残疾或过早死亡可能损失多少健康年数。报告发现，将全球升温限制在约1.8摄氏度而非4.4摄氏度，可避免损失18.9亿健康寿命年。\n\n该委员会由35位专家组成，研究结果将在柏林世界卫生峰会上分享。评估将每年更新。作者敦促领导人在风险演变成危机前采取行动，投资技术创新并加强卫生系统。他们表示，今天做出的决定将塑造下一代的健康。", "text": "A new report by the Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health warns that humans could die out by 2100. The two biggest dangers are the malicious use of AI, including biological weapons, and nuclear conflict, which could block sunlight and cause famine. The report says these risks are still unlikely, but their possible effects would be apocalyptic. The commission identified 17 major threats to human health over the next 75 years. Besides AI and nuclear war, the list includes air pollution, antimicrobial resistance, childhood malnutrition, climate breakdown, declining overseas aid and high alcohol use. It also names high blood pressure, income inequality, low education, pandemics, obesity, poor mental health, smoking and unhealthy ageing. To be included, each threat had to cause many lost healthy years and also have possible solutions. The experts modelled how many years of good health might be lost due to sickness, disability or early death. Limiting global heating to about 1.8C instead of 4.4C could prevent 1.89 billion years of healthy life being lost, the report found. The commission was made up of 35 experts, and its findings will be shared at the World Health Summit in Berlin. The assessment will be updated every year. The authors urged leaders to act before risks become crises, by investing in technology and strengthening health systems. Decisions made today, they say, will shape the health of the next generation.", "vocab": [{"word": "extinct", "pos": "adj", "zh": "灭绝的", "note": "指物种或人类完全消失", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "malicious", "pos": "adj", "zh": "恶意的", "note": "常与 use 搭配，表示故意造成伤害", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "assessment", "pos": "noun", "zh": "评估", "note": "指对情况的正式判断", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "threat", "pos": "noun", "zh": "威胁", "note": "常用搭配：a threat to health", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "famine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "饥荒", "note": "指大范围严重缺乏食物", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "apocalyptic", "pos": "adj", "zh": "灾难性的；世界末日的", "note": "形容极其严重的破坏", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "antimicrobial resistance", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "抗微生物药物耐药性", "note": "医学常用术语", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "malnutrition", "pos": "noun", "zh": "营养不良", "note": "常指儿童因食物不足导致的健康问题", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "model", "pos": "verb", "zh": "模拟；建立模型", "note": "此处指用数据预测未来情况", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "disability-adjusted life years", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "伤残调整生命年", "note": "衡量健康损失的指标，缩写为 Dalys", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "urge", "pos": "verb", "zh": "敦促；力劝", "note": "常用结构：urge somebody to do something", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "anticipation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "预测；预先准备", "note": "指提前想到并应对未来风险", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Nuclear war and malicious use of AI threaten end of humanity by 2100, says Lancet assessment", "summary_original": "Newly established commission identifies 17 catastrophic threats to human health but says ‘realistic route to act’ on each exists\nHumans could become extinct by the year 2100 due to malicious use of AI or nuclear conflict, according to a new global risk assessment.\nThey top a list of 17 major threats to human health over the next 75 years, which also includes cuts to aid spending by wealthy countries, the climate crisis and childhood malnutrition.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "Nuclear war and malicious use of AI threaten end of humanity by 2100, says Lancet assessment\nNewly established commission identifies 17 catastrophic threats to human health but says ‘realistic route to act’ on each exists\nHumans could become extinct by the year 2100 due to malicious use of AI or nuclear conflict, according to a new global risk assessment.\nThey top a list of 17 major threats to human health over the next 75 years, which also includes cuts to aid spending by wealthy countries, the climate crisis and childhood malnutrition.\nThe experts behind\nThe Lancet Commission on 21st Century Global Threats to Health\nsaid all the threats interact, meaning they must be addressed together – but stressed that there was “a realistic route to act” on each.\n“Decisions made by leaders today will shape the health and wellbeing of the next generation,” said Prof Christopher Murray, commission co-chair and the director of the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation, University of Washington.\nTo create its list, the commission modelled how many years in good health might be lost due to sickness, disability or early deaths due to each threat, based on current trajectories – a measure known as disability-adjusted life years (Dalys).\n“Malicious use of AI, including bioweapons, and nuclear conflict with nuclear famine carry risks of extinction in the next 75 years,” the report said, adding that while their likelihood remained low, they had the potential to end humanity in the case of nuclear conflict by injecting enough particles into the upper atmosphere to block sunlight and cause famine, or via AI-created biological weapons. An accompanying Lancet editorial, published alongside the report, warned: “The scale of harm, should they be realised, would inflict an apocalypse on humanity.”\nOther risks that could cause more than 1bn cumulative Dalys, and therefore deemed “major catastrophic threats”, included\nair pollution\n,\nantimicrobial resistance\n,\nchildhood malnutrition\nand climate breakdown, as well as conventional conflict,\ndeclining overseas aid\n,\nhigh alcohol use\n, high blood pressure, income inequality, low educational attainment,\nnaturally occurring pandemics\n,\nobesity\n, poor\nmental health\n,\nsmoking\nand unhealthy\nageing\n.\nTo be included on the list, threats not only had to potentially cause a high number of lost healthy years, but also to have potential solutions.\nIbrahim Abubakar, commission member and professor of infectious disease epidemiology at UCL, said: “We are not starting from a point of view of ‘oh look, we want to scare you – we’ve got X many threats facing humanity’. We’re starting from the premise that if you are a policymaker today, whether at the global multilateral level or at the national level, these are the potential threats that you could face, and this is the magnitude by which, if you act, you can actually make a difference.”\nAction to limit\nglobal heating to about 1.8C (3.2F)\nrather than letting it reach 4.4C could prevent 1.89bn years of healthy life being lost, according to the report, which modelled best and worst case scenarios for each threat.\nIn higher income regions, non-communicable diseases such as obesity and poor mental health were the dominant threats, while in lower income regions health loss was driven by issues such as low educational attainment.\nMany major donor nations, including the US and UK, have announced\nlarge cuts to aid spending\nover the past year. The potential collapse of, or steep reduction in, international health aid could cause up to 1.5bn Dalys before 2100, the commission found, nearly 70% of which would be in sub-Saharan Africa. The region also bears the brunt of child malnutrition.\nThe commission was made up of 35 experts, and its findings will be shared on Sunday at the World Health Summit in Berlin, Germany. Their assessment will be updated annually to re-evaluate the 17 threats identified, incorporating new data and any emerging hazards.\nThe authors urged leaders to address the risks before they escalated into crises, including via investment in technological innovations and by strengthening health systems.\n“We started this process three years ago, thinking that there were three big threats: conflict, climate, and pandemics,” said Murray. “There’s a much larger number of threats that we need to worry about, and governments really need to dedicate some money and staff to the task of anticipation, prevention and preparation for this more diverse set of threats.”\nExplore more on these topics\nGlobal health\nAI (artificial intelligence)\nNuclear weapons\nClimate crisis\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-11T00:18:05.945804", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "A", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.213, "sm": 100, "em": 212, "cs": 0, "ce": 1, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "new", "s": 0.225, "e": 0.463, "sm": 225, "em": 462, "cs": 2, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "report", "s": 0.475, "e": 0.938, "sm": 475, "em": 937, "cs": 6, "ce": 12, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "by", "s": 0.95, "e": 1.075, "sm": 950, "em": 1075, "cs": 13, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "the", "s": 1.087, "e": 1.2, "sm": 1087, "em": 1200, "cs": 16, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "Lancet", "s": 1.212, "e": 1.6, "sm": 1212, "em": 1599, "cs": 20, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "Commission", "s": 1.613, "e": 2.075, "sm": 1612, "em": 2075, "cs": 27, "ce": 37, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "on", "s": 2.087, "e": 2.225, "sm": 2087, "em": 2225, "cs": 38, "ce": 40, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "21", "s": 2.237, "e": 2.556, "sm": 2237, "em": 2556, "cs": 41, "ce": 43, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "st", "s": 2.556, "e": 2.875, "sm": 2556, "em": 2875, "cs": 43, "ce": 45, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "Century", "s": 2.888, "e": 3.35, "sm": 2887, "em": 3350, "cs": 46, "ce": 53, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "Global", "s": 3.362, "e": 3.737, "sm": 3362, "em": 3737, "cs": 54, "ce": 60, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "Threats", "s": 3.75, "e": 4.175, "sm": 3750, "em": 4175, "cs": 61, "ce": 68, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": "to", "s": 4.188, "e": 4.287, "sm": 4187, "em": 4287, "cs": 69, "ce": 71, "si": 0}, {"i": 14, "w": "Health", "s": 4.3, "e": 4.562, "sm": 4300, "em": 4562, "cs": 72, "ce": 78, "si": 0}, {"i": 15, "w": "warns", "s": 4.575, "e": 4.95, "sm": 4575, "em": 4950, "cs": 79, "ce": 84, "si": 0}, {"i": 16, "w": "that", "s": 4.963, "e": 5.088, "sm": 4962, "em": 5087, "cs": 85,